lifeos-nix patch f-pulse-unit-nixos-skip — on NixOS, defer the Pulse systemd unit to the nix module

PROBLEM
  PULSE/manage.sh (install action, Linux branch) templates a ~/.config/systemd/user/
  com.lifeos.pulse.service from the public template, whose PATH is
  __HOME__/.bun/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin. On NixOS none of those dirs hold
  bash or bun, so Bun.which("bash") returns null, spawnScript falls back to the absent
  "/bin/bash", and every cron job that shells out dies with ENOENT (cost-aggregation,
  healthcheck, poller-meta-monitor stuck since the 2026-08-09 migration; /healthz
  degraded). The lifeos-nix nixosModule now owns this unit declaratively with a correct
  `path` (bash/bun/git), but a user-level ~/.config unit SHADOWS the nix-provided one
  (load precedence ~/.config/systemd/user > /etc/systemd/user), so the installer must
  not write its own on NixOS — and must evict any stale one a pre-fix install left.

FIX (1 file)
  manage.sh — in the Linux install branch, detect NixOS (/etc/NIXOS) and: skip the unit
  templating + `systemctl --user enable`; `rm -f` any stale ~/.config unit (else it
  shadows the nix one and no test catches it — the daemon still binds :31337, only cron
  spawnScript dies); enable-linger, daemon-reload, start the nix-managed unit. Non-NixOS
  Linux is unchanged (still templates + enables its own unit). macOS branch untouched.

diff --git a/LifeOS/install/LIFEOS/PULSE/manage.sh b/LifeOS/install/LIFEOS/PULSE/manage.sh
--- a/LifeOS/install/LIFEOS/PULSE/manage.sh
+++ b/LifeOS/install/LIFEOS/PULSE/manage.sh
@@ -116,14 +116,27 @@
       systemctl --user stop "$PLIST_NAME" 2>/dev/null || true
       pkill -9 -f "bun.*pulse.ts" 2>/dev/null || true
       sleep 1
-      # Substitute __HOME__ + __BUN_PATH__ placeholders (public template);
-      # no-op on service files that already have literal paths.
-      sed -e "s|__HOME__|$HOME|g" -e "s|__BUN_PATH__|$BUN_PATH|g" "$SERVICE_SRC" > "$SERVICE_DST"
-      # Ensure user services survive logout/reboot (no-op if already enabled)
-      loginctl enable-linger "$USER" 2>/dev/null || true
-      systemctl --user daemon-reload
-      systemctl --user enable "$PLIST_NAME"
-      systemctl --user start "$PLIST_NAME"
+      if [ -e /etc/NIXOS ]; then
+        # NixOS: the com.lifeos.pulse.service unit is owned declaratively by the
+        # lifeos-nix nixosModule (correct PATH with bash/bun via `path`). A
+        # user-level ~/.config unit would SHADOW the nix one (load precedence
+        # ~/.config/systemd/user > /etc/systemd/user) and reintroduce the
+        # /usr/bin:/bin PATH (no bash → every cron job ENOENTs on /bin/bash), so
+        # remove any stale one a pre-fix install left, then start the nix unit.
+        rm -f "$SERVICE_DST"
+        loginctl enable-linger "$USER" 2>/dev/null || true
+        systemctl --user daemon-reload
+        systemctl --user start "$PLIST_NAME"
+      else
+        # Substitute __HOME__ + __BUN_PATH__ placeholders (public template);
+        # no-op on service files that already have literal paths.
+        sed -e "s|__HOME__|$HOME|g" -e "s|__BUN_PATH__|$BUN_PATH|g" "$SERVICE_SRC" > "$SERVICE_DST"
+        # Ensure user services survive logout/reboot (no-op if already enabled)
+        loginctl enable-linger "$USER" 2>/dev/null || true
+        systemctl --user daemon-reload
+        systemctl --user enable "$PLIST_NAME"
+        systemctl --user start "$PLIST_NAME"
+      fi
     else
       # Cleanup any prior pulse before installing fresh — prevents the stale-PID
       # / unbound-port half-dead state where a previous launchd-managed pulse is
